/*! 
\page GEOMEC_INTRO Introduction

Geomec is 3D and 2D compaction special to perform compaction analysis on oil or gas reservoirs. Geomec is develop for Shell by TNO Diana BV. This page is a short introduction to the Geomec and it's software architecture.
Geomec models are based on finite element analysis. Geomec 3D allows the user to generate a finite element mesh based on import surfaces. Geomec supports tetrahedron and hexahedron meshes. A \ref GEOMEC_2D model is generated by intersecting a 3D \ref GEOMEC_HEXA or \ref GEOMEC_TETRA .

\section GEOMEC_SHORT Geomec at a glance

The mesh (CMeshBase) is generated by horizons (CHorizonBase). These horizons represent the geological layers in the geo-mechanical model. The horizons could have slipping capabillities (CHorizonBase::Slip). The model demarcated by the (CBoundaryBase). The elements generated by the mesher are split up to compartements or bodies (IFormationElementSet). These compartments are assigned to formations (CFormationBase). A formation can consist of multiple compartments containing the elements. Interface elements (geo::IInterfaceElement) are generated for the slipping horizons.
The formation (CFormationBase) defines the geological features of a layer. The formation has a reference to a material (IMaterial) and a pore pressure (CPressure) on the formation could be defined for each time step (CDepletionStage). The formation will follow the initial pressure (CGlobalPressure) by default. The formation becomes a reservoir when the user defines a pressure (CPressure) on the formation.

A mesh must be generated, a material must be assigned to each formation and at least one formation must be defined as a reservoir to perform an analysis. The user can check these criteria for analysis in the analysis criteria dialog (CModelValidationDlg). Geomec can perform a linear (CGeomecDoc::OnAnalysisLinearDepletion) and a non-linear analysis (CGeomecDoc::OnAnalysisNonlinearCalculation). The settings for the analysis could be set in the calculation property dialog (CCalculationProperties). Additional parameters for non-linear analysis could be found in the non-linear parameter dialog (CNonlinParams). Geomec directly writes the model on the Filos file by using CGeomecDianaRunnerBase. 
Geomec can only generated first order meshes, but Geomec allows analysis based on quadratic elements. Geomec call the routine 'mkridge.c' to convert the linear elements to quadratic elements. 

After the analysis is carried out the results are obtained from the filos file by the CResultRegister. The CResultRegister stores the results in the memory and cashes the results on the disk when the operating system is running out of memory. The CResultRegister only stores the basic results obtained from the filos file. The time steps (CDepletionStage) and the mesh are associated to the result register, because a result is stored for an geo::IElement, a CDepletionStage and for an analysis type (Linear or Non-Linear).
The result tree (CResultTree) shows results derived from the basic results in the CResultRegister. The result tree consists of a CResultGroup, a IResult and a IResultComponent constellation. The CResultGroup is composite owning CResultGroups and IResults. The IResult is owning IResultComponents. The user can view IResults and IResultComponents directly in the COpenGLValueScene.

\section GEOMEC_INPUT Geomec input

The imported input data is located at the data storage tree (CDataTreeView). Input data is obtained from Excel (CElementSetExcel), ASCII (CElementSetFile) and GoCad (CGocadFile). The user is able to import point sets (CPointSet), element sets (CElementSet) and surface(CSurfaceBase) in 3D. Point sets and element set contain set of values (IValueSet) in every node (CNodalValueSet) or element (CElementValueSet). Using point- and elements sets the user can define values distributed in space. The users can also define values in a formula (CRpnValueSet) by using the RPN calculator(CRPNDlg).
The user could attach the values from the point- and element sets to a CValueComponent. The CValueComponent is a component of the CValueType representing a quantity produced in the CValueTypeFactory. The assignment of IValueSet to CValueComponent and the creation of the CValueType is done in the CAttriPointSetTemplate.
The CValueType objects could be assigned to CGlobalPressure, CPressure, CGlobalInitialStressNode, CGlobalTemperature and CMaterialServer located by observers (CNodeObsever etc) in the model tree (CModelTreeView).
The CElementSetFile and CElementSetExcelFile share input parsers based on the base class IImportFormat. The IImportFormat class defines the generic import interface and regulates the assignment of IValueSet and CValueType to the IPointSet class. The CNodalImportFormat import CPointSet objects, the CElementImportFormat imports CElementSet objects, the CResultImport format imports result files from another kernel and finally the CWellPath format imports CWellPath objects.

\subsection GEOMEC_MATERIAL Materials
The material architecture is powered by an old OCX MatLibX descending of the Geomec 2.8 era. The MatlibX can edit material parameters and calibrate material models. The MatlibX module is embedded in the CMaterialEntry containing the IMaterial instances. The IMaterial has material parameters (CMaterialParameter) corresponding to the parameters handled by the OCX. The values defined by CMaterialParameter are constant values.
The IMaterial is attached to the CMaterialServer. An IMaterial is allowed to be assigned to multiple CMaterialServers. The user could also attach distrbruted values (CValueType) to the CMaterialServer. The material server generates a CFFMaterial for each element in the mesh when distributed values (CValueType) are assigned to the CMaterialServer. The material models supported by Geomec are derived from IMaterial. The super class of IMaterial implements the filos writing routine based on a CFFMaterial. The IRockMaterial is assigned to the material definition of formations (CFormationBase) and the CCasingGrade is assigned to the material definition of a CWellPath.

\section GEOMEC_OUTPUT Geomec output

The output of the analysis is stored in the CResultRegister and is accessible by the CStressTensorValueSet class, the CStrainTensorValueSet class, the CVectorValueSet class and the CScalarValueSet class. 

Geomec supports a wide range of export functionality. The input data read in by file, all the deratives of IPointSet, could be exported by the CElementSetFile or the CElementSetFileExcel. Similarly to the import the export is also based on a IExportFormat base class to allow CElementSetFile and CElementSetFileExcel share the export funcitonality. The CNodalExportFormat allows to export CPointSet and CWellPath objects. The CElementSetExportFormat allows the export generic IElementSet object, the CFormationBase objects, the CHorizonBase objects, the CCrossSection objects and finially the export of the basic results of the CResultTree.
The user interface of the export of the CResultTree is located in the cResultExportDlg. The IExportCatagory defines the groups objects to export and IResultGroupObserver defines the CResultTree. Additionally it is also possible to export 





